WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.070
Narrator: Lions have
them, hippos have them,

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.320
and even you and I have them: canines,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.100
those long, pointy front teeth.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.493
They're actually the longest
teeth in the human mouth.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.060
So what are these long, sharp teeth

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.810
doing among our short, stubby ones?

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.290
Well, contrary to popular belief,

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.370
it's not for tearing and ripping meat.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:30.283
The real reason is actually
much more romantic than that.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.090
Human men today have 10%
longer canines than women,

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.000
and this difference isn't
unique to our species.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.410
Our close relatives, gorillas, have

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.560
it as well to an even larger extent.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.720
Males' canines are twice
as long as females'.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.100
That's because in gorilla society,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.840
males compete for
exclusive mating rights to

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.670
the entire female troop, and the male

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.790
with the longest, most intimidating

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.620
set of fangs usually wins.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.453
Over time, gorillas have evolved

00:01:06.453 --> 00:01:07.286
increasingly longer canines,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.030
but when it comes to humans, our teeth

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.490
took a different evolutionary path.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.620
Our canines have actually
become shorter over time.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.140
Just look at the canines on

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.913
the first human, Ardipithecus ramidus,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.540
or Australopithecus anamensis
versus modern humans.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.950
In fact, our canines
today are the shortest

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.450
they've ever been, and the difference

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.400
between male and female canines

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.910
got less pronounced as well.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.400
That's because unlike modern gorillas

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.230
who take after our
shared ancient ancestors,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.580
human men eventually stopped fighting

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.520
with their teeth somewhere along the way.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.010
Now, scientists aren't entirely
sure why this happened,

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.440
but one possibility is that our babies

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.590
grew increasingly defenseless, so males

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.970
had to spend more time on childcare

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.973
and less time on winning a mate.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.800
As a result, all that's
left in our mouths today

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.670
is the memory of a more slobbery method

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.650
of one-upping our peers.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.040
That's right, those canines you carry

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.140
around today aren't particularly useful.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.330
For one thing, they're not long enough

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.410
to grab and hold on to prey
the way lions use them,

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.320
and they're not big enough to intimidate

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.570
predators' arrivals the
way hippos use them.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.860
Instead, they help us
bite into food. That's it.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.800
Turns out, evolution isn't always useful,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.790
but while the truth about our canines

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.260
might be disappointing, those teeth

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.870
are still pretty amazing because

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.670
anthropologists use canine size and shape

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.380
to help track when
humanlike ancestors evolved.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.073
So in a way, our tiny
canines make us who we are.

